better than no movie.

230
00:10:42,343 --> 00:10:43,744
Exactly.

231
00:10:47,815 --> 00:10:49,216
<i>I've decided to roll the dice</i>

232
00:10:49,316 --> 00:10:51,719
<i>and call it quits</i>
<i>on my corporate video career.</i>

233
00:10:54,722 --> 00:10:56,591
<i>So I'm chasing this dream</i>

234
00:10:56,690 --> 00:10:59,660
<i>one last time.</i>

235
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:02,696
<i>I need to know</i>
<i>what this company sees in me.</i>

236
00:11:02,796 --> 00:11:04,865
<i>And why no-one else saw it.</i>

237
00:11:14,008 --> 00:11:15,843
-[knocking on door]
-[man] Yeah?

238
00:11:17,912 --> 00:11:19,780
-David?
-Oh! Hey.

239
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:21,682
-Nice to finally meet you.
-Oh!

240
00:11:21,782 --> 00:11:22,750
-Uh... Anthony!
-Yeah.

241
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:25,554
-Oh, hey! How's it going?
-Yeah, good.

242
00:11:25,654 --> 00:11:28,789
-Uh... You're in from Australia.
-Yes, yep.

243
00:11:28,889 --> 00:11:30,958
-Have you seen The Asylum?
-No.

244
00:11:31,058 --> 00:11:32,527
-Do you wanna see The Asylum?
-Yeah. I'd love to.

245
00:11:32,627 --> 00:11:35,362
[David]
We're shooting an alien movie.

246
00:11:35,462 --> 00:11:38,399
So you'll fit right at home.

247
00:11:38,499 --> 00:11:41,503
Uh... We have random people
all over the place.

248
00:11:41,603 --> 00:11:44,271
-Mostly homeless.
-[laughs]

249
00:11:44,371 --> 00:11:46,207
Don't talk to them.
Don't look people in the eye.

250
00:11:46,307 --> 00:11:48,709
I don't know
half the people here.

251
00:11:52,980 --> 00:11:54,982
..everything, for God's sake.

252
00:11:56,917 --> 00:11:58,653
[man] Action!

253
00:11:58,786 --> 00:12:00,655
We make shitty movies

254
00:12:00,754 --> 00:12:02,591
for people with bad taste

255
00:12:02,691 --> 00:12:03,991
and alcoholics.

256
00:12:04,091 --> 00:12:08,796
The Asylum has
produced over 300 movies now.

257
00:12:08,896 --> 00:12:13,133
Brian Grazer called us
"the pollution of Hollywood".

258
00:12:13,234 --> 00:12:15,503
You know, you just
have to kind of deal with it

259
00:12:15,604 --> 00:12:16,937
but you don't want it.

260
00:12:17,037 --> 00:12:19,541
So we're, I guess, annoying.

261
00:12:20,241 --> 00:12:23,244
[Paul Bales] I don't think that
Hollywood knows that we exist.

262
00:12:23,344 --> 00:12:27,047
And so we very much
fly under the radar.

263
00:12:29,183 --> 00:12:33,287
We're... you know,
barely above porn.

264
00:12:35,022 --> 00:12:36,757
Well, come on back here.

265
00:12:36,857 --> 00:12:39,460
[David] Oh, look at this.
We're in command central.

266
00:12:39,561 --> 00:12:41,128
[Anthony] The crown jewel?

267
00:12:41,228 --> 00:12:43,864
Alright,
so you probably wanna know

268
00:12:43,964 --> 00:12:45,165
what movie <i>you're</i> gonna direct.

269
00:12:45,266 --> 00:12:47,835
Alright, you're
gonna be directing a film

270
00:12:47,935 --> 00:12:50,404
called
'The Land That Time Forgot'.

271
00:12:50,505 --> 00:12:52,973
-Okay.
-Alright.

272
00:12:53,073 --> 00:12:54,643
It is what we call
a six-day special.

273
00:12:54,743 --> 00:12:57,144
And it's called
a six-day special because

274
00:12:57,244 --> 00:12:59,547
you have six days
to make this movie.

275
00:12:59,648 --> 00:13:01,348
-Alright?
-Okay.

276
00:13:03,183 --> 00:13:04,451
Yeah.

277
00:13:04,552 --> 00:13:05,853
When do we shoot?

278
00:13:05,953 --> 00:13:08,822
I think we're supposed
to be shooting in a few weeks.

279
00:13:08,922 --> 00:13:11,058
Now, that said, we don't
have a script at the moment.

280
00:13:11,158 --> 00:13:13,561
Although I think the script's
coming in in about a week.

281
00:13:13,662 --> 00:13:15,095
Okay. [laughs]

282
00:13:15,195 --> 00:13:18,399
Yeah. Uh... There's
probably a lot to discuss

283
00:13:18,499 --> 00:13:19,933
about how to do this thing.

284
00:13:20,034 --> 00:13:21,770
Why do you want to do this?

285
00:13:21,869 --> 00:13:24,104
Well, you gotta start somewhere.

286
00:13:24,204 --> 00:13:27,441
Now, we did make a movie
'The Land That Time Forgot'

287
00:13:27,542 --> 00:13:28,942
a number of years ago.

288
00:13:29,043 --> 00:13:31,378
You should take a look at that.
See how that went.

289
00:13:31,478 --> 00:13:34,516
[Anthony] Can I ask why you're
making the same movie again?

290
00:13:34,616 --> 00:13:38,018
That's the dumbest question
I ever heard. Wow.

291
00:13:38,118 --> 00:13:42,156
I'm not gonna even answer that
question 'coz that is stupid.

292
00:13:42,256 --> 00:13:45,225
'The Land That Time Forgot'

293
00:13:45,326 --> 00:13:49,430
is, obviously,
based on a classic story

294
00:13:49,531 --> 00:13:50,931
that's in the public domain

295
00:13:51,031 --> 00:13:53,967
and one that we
had adapted previously

296
00:13:54,068 --> 00:13:58,138
because anything with dinosaurs
is a go.

297
00:14:00,675 --> 00:14:03,177
[roars]

298
00:14:03,277 --> 00:14:04,813
[man] Go on!

299
00:14:04,912 --> 00:14:06,548
Nooooo!

300
00:14:06,648 --> 00:14:08,248
[Anthony, VO]
<i>I can't believe</i>

301
00:14:08,349 --> 00:14:10,451
<i>they make these movies</i>
<i>in six days...</i>

302
00:14:10,552 --> 00:14:12,119
[roars]

303
00:14:12,219 --> 00:14:14,054
<i>...without cutting any corners.</i>

304
00:14:17,224 --> 00:14:19,694
<i>Okay, maybe a few snips</i>
<i>here and there.</i>

305
00:14:19,794 --> 00:14:23,364
<i>And don't normal Hollywood</i>
<i>movies take months to shoot?</i>

306
00:14:23,464 --> 00:14:26,668
<i>Surely, more time</i>
<i>equals a better movie.</i>

307
00:14:26,768 --> 00:14:28,435
Why do you do this?

308
00:14:28,536 --> 00:14:30,505
[laughs] Money.

309
00:14:30,605 --> 00:14:31,840
It's real simple math.

310
00:14:31,939 --> 00:14:34,007
We know how much
we're gonna sell the film for,

311
00:14:34,108 --> 00:14:36,845
and my partners say, "Don't
make it for more than this."

312
00:14:36,944 --> 00:14:38,979
When the revenue
from one movie comes in

313
00:14:39,079 --> 00:14:41,348
we use it to finance
the next movie.

314
00:14:41,448 --> 00:14:44,017
[Paul Bales]
So, as a result of this

315
00:14:44,118 --> 00:14:47,689
we have, for the most part,
never lost money on a movie.

316
00:14:47,789 --> 00:14:49,022
Look out!

317
00:14:49,123 --> 00:14:51,291
The trick is really
just to make the movie

318
00:14:51,392 --> 00:14:54,361
for less than
you're gonna earn.

319
00:14:54,461 --> 00:14:57,030
A regular Hollywood movie
will cost

320
00:14:57,131 --> 00:14:58,767
anywhere from
30 million dollars

321
00:14:58,867 --> 00:15:00,835
to a quarter
of a billion dollars

322
00:15:00,934 --> 00:15:02,936
whereas an Asylum movie

323
00:15:03,036 --> 00:15:04,706
will cost
a quarter million dollars

324
00:15:04,806 --> 00:15:06,340
to about a million dollars.

325
00:15:10,712 --> 00:15:14,749
The studios, in my opinion,
make very shitty movies.

326
00:15:14,849 --> 00:15:17,151
But they make very shitty movies

327
00:15:17,251 --> 00:15:19,420
for millions and millions
of dollars.

328
00:15:19,521 --> 00:15:21,689
We make shitty movies

329
00:15:21,790 --> 00:15:23,090
but we make them
at the right price

330
00:15:23,190 --> 00:15:25,025
and the right length -
90 minutes -

331
00:15:25,125 --> 00:15:27,729
which is as long
as any movie should be.

332
00:15:27,829 --> 00:15:30,732
I'm talking to you,
James Cameron.

333
00:15:31,231 --> 00:15:36,805
And then everything else can be
shot over each other's shoulder.

334
00:15:36,905 --> 00:15:39,072
We're trying to populate,
like, a scene.

335
00:15:39,173 --> 00:15:42,644
And, because of the glasses
thing you've got

336
00:15:42,744 --> 00:15:48,182
would you mind helping us out
being maybe, like, a nurse?

337
00:15:50,384 --> 00:15:51,886
[Anthony, VO]
<i>I've never felt comfortable</i>

338
00:15:52,019 --> 00:15:53,755
<i>being in front of the camera.</i>

339
00:15:53,855 --> 00:15:55,255
171 Alpha take one. Mark.

340
00:15:55,355 --> 00:15:58,927
<i>But at least I can see how</i>
<i>this all works from the inside.</i>

341
00:15:59,026 --> 00:16:00,494
Can't believe
you two got here so fast.

342
00:16:00,595 --> 00:16:02,296
[man] We were right by
an air base, Dad.

343
00:16:02,396 --> 00:16:04,498
It was easy to hitch a ride.

344
00:16:05,733 --> 00:16:07,267
<i>While it's my first role,</i>

345
00:16:07,367 --> 00:16:10,304
<i>my co-star has been</i>
<i>in over 250 movies.</i>

346
00:16:10,404 --> 00:16:12,907
That is my understanding
at this time.

347
00:16:13,006 --> 00:16:14,441
That means there
must be some way

348
00:16:14,542 --> 00:16:18,111
to disrupt their
electromagnetic field.

349
00:16:20,915 --> 00:16:21,883
[gunshot]

350
00:16:21,982 --> 00:16:23,116
[man] Action!

351
00:16:23,217 --> 00:16:24,719
<i>It's intimidating</i>
<i>to be in the presence</i>

352
00:16:24,819 --> 00:16:26,487
<i>of Asylum royalty.</i>

353
00:16:26,588 --> 00:16:27,354
-How'd I do?
-Hmm?

354
00:16:27,454 --> 00:16:29,757
-How'd I do?
-You did good.

355
00:16:29,858 --> 00:16:32,894
Looked like you'd been wrapping
up fractured hands for a while.

356
00:16:32,993 --> 00:16:34,094
[laughs] Yeah, great.

357
00:16:34,194 --> 00:16:35,964
You're also right.

358
00:16:36,063 --> 00:16:38,165
It didn't matter that
there were aliens inside.

359
00:16:38,265 --> 00:16:41,536
<i>But now I see how they</i>
<i>make these movies in six days.</i>

360
00:16:41,636 --> 00:16:43,938
I can't believe
you two got here so fast.

361
00:16:44,037 --> 00:16:46,106
<i>I thought this would be chaos.</i>

362
00:16:46,206 --> 00:16:49,343
<i>But this crew,</i>
<i>they knew their stuff.</i>

363
00:16:49,443 --> 00:16:51,044
Cut!

364
00:16:51,144 --> 00:16:53,948
Ladies and gentlemen,
that is a wrap on Day Five!

365
00:16:54,047 --> 00:16:57,150
[applause and cheering]

366
00:16:57,251 --> 00:17:00,120
Can I have your autograph?
You did awesome, man. Thank you.

367
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:01,956
-Yeah, you did good!
-I tried.

368
00:17:02,055 --> 00:17:03,658
Did good for short notice, man.

369
00:17:04,959 --> 00:17:06,961
<i>If they're blown away</i>
<i>by my acting</i>

370
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:08,763
<i>wait until they see me direct.</i>

371
00:17:14,636 --> 00:17:16,236
[stirring music plays]

372
00:17:16,336 --> 00:17:19,172
<i>My first Asylum experience</i>
<i>was a real eye opener.</i>

373
00:17:19,273 --> 00:17:21,041
[man] <i>Can't believe</i>
<i>you two got here so fast.</i>

374
00:17:21,141 --> 00:17:22,409
We were right by an air base,
Dad.

375
00:17:22,510 --> 00:17:24,579
<i>Hopefully, I made the final cut.</i>

376
00:17:26,548 --> 00:17:28,382
<i>But I still have</i>
<i>a lot of questions</i>

377
00:17:28,482 --> 00:17:29,851
<i>about the film</i> I'm <i>making.</i>

378
00:17:29,951 --> 00:17:32,252
<i>Like, what happens in it?</i>

379
00:17:32,352 --> 00:17:34,022
<i>Instead of a script</i>

380
00:17:34,121 --> 00:17:35,623
<i>I've been given</i>
<i>this piece of paper</i>

381
00:17:35,723 --> 00:17:38,325
<i>with a few key plot points</i>
<i>and character names on it.</i>

382
00:17:38,425 --> 00:17:41,829
[man] So, you read the
one-pager. What did you think?

383
00:17:41,930 --> 00:17:44,866
[laughs]

384
00:17:44,966 --> 00:17:47,702
Um... Yeah.
It's not what I expected.

385
00:17:50,004 --> 00:17:51,639
I mean, finding
a jungle might be hard.

386
00:17:51,739 --> 00:17:53,307
Yeah.

387
00:17:53,407 --> 00:17:55,843
There's two submarines
and a U-boat.

388
00:17:55,944 --> 00:17:57,845
Are you panicked yet?

389
00:17:57,946 --> 00:18:00,014
A little more so, now.

390
00:18:00,113 --> 00:18:01,516
Okay.

391
00:18:01,616 --> 00:18:04,786
It'll be a fun movie,
though. It'll be cool.

392
00:18:04,886 --> 00:18:08,155
Just know the story in and out.

393
00:18:08,255 --> 00:18:10,157
I've seen a lot of directors

394
00:18:10,257 --> 00:18:13,260
try and,
you know, kind of say,

395
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:18,265
"I am Scorsese and this movie
will be amazing and awesome."

396
00:18:18,365 --> 00:18:19,867
And they'll fight the process.

397
00:18:19,968 --> 00:18:21,435
And it's, like,
you can't do that.

398
00:18:21,536 --> 00:18:23,203
If you go with the process,
you have a chance

399
00:18:23,303 --> 00:18:25,272
to make a fun Asylum film.

400
00:18:25,372 --> 00:18:28,643
If you fight it, your movie
will be clunky and weird.

401
00:18:28,743 --> 00:18:30,078
You know, all that.

402
00:18:30,177 --> 00:18:32,145
So, honestly, you'll be fine.

403
00:18:33,213 --> 00:18:34,481
<i>But I want this to be</i> my <i>film.</i>

404
00:18:34,582 --> 00:18:37,250
<i>It sounds like</i>
<i>they just want it</i>

405
00:18:37,351 --> 00:18:38,820
<i>to look like</i>
<i>everything else they make.</i>

406
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:41,154
<i>This isn't what I dreamt of.</i>

407
00:18:41,254 --> 00:18:42,724
[Brendan] Good morning.

408
00:18:42,824 --> 00:18:44,659
I can't stay awake.

409
00:18:48,663 --> 00:18:51,766
<i>Honestly, I thought</i>
<i>I'd be able to do this my way.</i>

410
00:18:51,866 --> 00:18:53,601
<i>That's why I came here.</i>

411
00:18:53,701 --> 00:18:57,437
<i>But now I'm worried if I enter</i>
<i>the trash cinema basement</i>

412
00:18:57,538 --> 00:18:59,406
<i>I'll smell like it forever.</i>

413
00:19:00,608 --> 00:19:02,577
<i>So it's time to talk to someone</i>

414
00:19:02,677 --> 00:19:04,646
<i>whose work ranges</i>
<i>from the bargain bin</i>

415
00:19:04,746 --> 00:19:05,880
<i>to the box office.</i>

416
00:19:05,980 --> 00:19:09,884
<i>A man who's an Asylum regular</i>
<i>and an Oscar nominee.</i>

417
00:19:13,186 --> 00:19:15,355
What are you drinking? Your tea?

418
00:19:15,455 --> 00:19:17,224
Rum.

419
00:19:18,225 --> 00:19:20,828
Anyway. [laughs]

420
00:19:21,596 --> 00:19:23,430
<i>Eric's a prolific Hollywood icon</i>

421
00:19:23,531 --> 00:19:26,100
<i>with over 800 movies</i>
<i>to his name</i>

422
00:19:26,199 --> 00:19:28,301
<i>Including 50 Asylum titles.</i>

423
00:19:28,402 --> 00:19:29,570
I hope you have good news.

424
00:19:29,671 --> 00:19:31,706
<i>If there is an upcoming movie</i>
<i>you want to see</i>

425
00:19:31,806 --> 00:19:33,340
<i>chances are he's in it.</i>

426
00:19:36,511 --> 00:19:38,211
I'm actually directing a film
for The Asylum.

427
00:19:38,311 --> 00:19:39,881
And it's my
first ever feature film.

428
00:19:39,981 --> 00:19:41,281
-Congratulations!
-Yeah.

429
00:19:41,381 --> 00:19:42,850
When you say
your first feature

430
00:19:42,950 --> 00:19:44,317
what have you been doing?

431
00:19:44,418 --> 00:19:46,219
Music videos, commercials?

432
00:19:46,319 --> 00:19:48,556
Yeah, some short films,
corporate videos.

433
00:19:48,656 --> 00:19:50,490
If they're gonna hand you
a project and say go,

434
00:19:50,591 --> 00:19:52,660
they mean it's your project.

435
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:53,528
Go.

436
00:19:53,628 --> 00:19:56,430
And they're really cool
that way.

437
00:19:56,531 --> 00:19:59,299
But it's a lot of pressure
on you.

438
00:19:59,399 --> 00:20:00,935
Keep talking,
I'll be right back.

439
00:20:01,035 --> 00:20:04,138
What? Eric?! We're filming!

440
00:20:04,237 --> 00:20:06,040
I just wanna change glasses.

441
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:07,307
Oh, okay.

442
00:20:07,407 --> 00:20:08,609
It's okay. I'm right here.

443
00:20:08,710 --> 00:20:12,013
I think you'll
get a lot of support.

444
00:20:12,113 --> 00:20:13,681
And also you can be creative.

445
00:20:13,781 --> 00:20:17,484
There's a format and a formula
but there aren't a lot of rules.

446
00:20:17,585 --> 00:20:19,486
[laughs]

447
00:20:19,587 --> 00:20:21,122
So you can really have fun.

448
00:20:21,221 --> 00:20:23,423
Now, Tolbert!

449
00:20:26,961 --> 00:20:29,197
If this thing turns back for us,
we're in trouble.

450
00:20:29,296 --> 00:20:32,100
'Coz you've been in huge-budget
Hollywood movies

451
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:33,735
and then tiny-budget
Asylum movies...

452
00:20:33,835 --> 00:20:36,070
what's the difference?

453
00:20:36,170 --> 00:20:38,039
There is no difference
for the actor.

454
00:20:38,139 --> 00:20:39,439
It's all the same process.

455
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:40,908
You do your homework at home.

456
00:20:41,008 --> 00:20:42,442
You arrive ready to go.

457
00:20:42,543 --> 00:20:44,846
You dress, go to make-up,
they tell you where to stand.

458
00:20:44,946 --> 00:20:47,014
And you act.

459
00:20:47,115 --> 00:20:49,884
Okay, you sons of bitches!

460
00:20:49,984 --> 00:20:51,953
Come and get me!

461
00:20:56,190 --> 00:20:58,025
The only real difference is

462
00:20:58,126 --> 00:20:59,894
if you're on 'Dark Knight'

463
00:20:59,994 --> 00:21:04,632
and you have the old zeppelin
hanger 30 miles north of London

464
00:21:04,732 --> 00:21:06,801
and it's all Gotham City
as you walk in,

465
00:21:06,901 --> 00:21:09,704
oh, my God!
It's a movie geek's paradise.

466
00:21:09,804 --> 00:21:12,640
As opposed to
a stool and a desk.

467
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:15,342
But that's the only difference.

468
00:21:15,442 --> 00:21:20,615
If I mess up this movie,
is that it for me, do you think?

469
00:21:20,715 --> 00:21:22,116
[Eliza] No!

470
00:21:22,216 --> 00:21:23,718
I don't think
you <i>can</i> screw it up.

471
00:21:23,818 --> 00:21:25,385
Just don't take it
too seriously.

472
00:21:25,485 --> 00:21:27,420
-Oh, you can screw it up.
-No, he can't.

473
00:21:27,522 --> 00:21:29,356
Nah, you can screw anything up.
But you're not going to.

474
00:21:29,456 --> 00:21:31,592
Because it's gonna be
so much fun for you.

475
00:21:31,692 --> 00:21:35,062
And you're gonna realize
that Orson Welles was right.

476
00:21:35,163 --> 00:21:36,531
It's a great, big train set

477
00:21:36,631 --> 00:21:39,332
and it's so much fun
to make it all go right.

478
00:21:39,432 --> 00:21:41,502
And... action.

479
00:21:43,838 --> 00:21:47,675
<i>If Asylum movies are good enough</i>
<i>for a legend like Eric Roberts</i>

480
00:21:47,775 --> 00:21:49,442
<i>then they're</i>
<i>definitely good enough</i>

481
00:21:49,544 --> 00:21:51,512
<i>for an unemployed</i>
<i>corporate video director.</i>

482
00:21:51,612 --> 00:21:53,181
<i>It is strange, though.</i>

483
00:21:53,281 --> 00:21:55,750
<i>The Asylum never</i>
<i>asked to see my work.</i>

484
00:21:55,850 --> 00:21:58,719
<i>But I guess you don't</i>
<i>make movies for decades</i>

485
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:00,721
<i>without having</i>
<i>an eye for spotting talent.</i>

486
00:22:00,822 --> 00:22:03,024
Considering
I've never made a movie

487
00:22:03,124 --> 00:22:05,226
why are you letting me
make one?

488
00:22:05,325 --> 00:22:09,429
It's because you asked.

489
00:22:09,797 --> 00:22:12,200
The trick for us
is to find people

490
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:14,367
that are new to the industry

491
00:22:14,467 --> 00:22:17,004
and don't know
that it's impossible

492
00:22:17,104 --> 00:22:19,173
to make a movie in six days.

493
00:22:19,273 --> 00:22:20,908
Let me find you a contract.

494
00:22:22,009 --> 00:22:23,443
Here.

495
00:22:23,544 --> 00:22:25,613
If you blow it
on an Asylum movie

496
00:22:25,713 --> 00:22:28,950
I think you've basically
crippled your career

497
00:22:29,050 --> 00:22:30,417
and that's on you.

498
00:22:30,518 --> 00:22:33,554
You clearly have
some mental problems

499
00:22:33,654 --> 00:22:35,990
that make you want to do this.

500
00:22:36,090 --> 00:22:37,424
And we get a movie out of it.

501
00:22:37,525 --> 00:22:39,660
Are you sure
you want to work with us?

502
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:42,263
-[laughs]
-[Anthony] I am.

503
00:22:42,362 --> 00:22:44,232
Alright, I'll make a copy.

504
00:22:44,332 --> 00:22:46,499
There you go.
Welcome to The Asylum.

505
00:22:56,777 --> 00:22:58,779
[Anthony, VO] <i>It's exciting</i>
<i>to be officially on board.</i>

506
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:01,414
<i>But apparently</i>
<i>I still have a lot to organize</i>

507
00:23:01,515 --> 00:23:02,783
<i>before I leave LA.</i>

508
00:23:02,884 --> 00:23:05,553
<i>So The Asylum</i>
<i>assigned Brendan as producer.</i>

509
00:23:05,653 --> 00:23:09,257
<i>His entire job is to stop me</i>
<i>from going off the rails.</i>

510
00:23:09,357 --> 00:23:11,424
You could use
the dead body up there.

511
00:23:11,525 --> 00:23:13,393
That's always beneficial.

512
00:23:14,528 --> 00:23:15,997
-You can have...
-A few guns.

513
00:23:16,097 --> 00:23:17,497
A few guns.

514
00:23:17,598 --> 00:23:19,767
I don't know.
Americans seem to like them.

515
00:23:19,867 --> 00:23:22,469
<i>He'll be supervising production</i>
<i>from LA.</i>

516
00:23:22,570 --> 00:23:23,971
<i>And, most importantly,</i>

517
00:23:24,071 --> 00:23:25,840
<i>he's in charge</i>
<i>of finding an actual star</i>

518
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:28,309
<i>who can make people</i>
<i>take this movie seriously.</i>

519
00:23:28,408 --> 00:23:29,944
-Yeah, it's got some heft.
-Right?

520
00:23:30,044 --> 00:23:32,914
[Brendan] So, when
it comes to casting this movie

521
00:23:33,014 --> 00:23:34,782
we have a list of names

522
00:23:34,882 --> 00:23:37,251
and we wanted your opinion
on a few people.

523
00:23:37,351 --> 00:23:40,888
So, Anthony here knows a whole
bunch of people in Australia

524
00:23:40,988 --> 00:23:44,325
so we want to know
if they'll work for this.

525
00:23:44,424 --> 00:23:45,826
I don't know them personally.

526
00:23:45,927 --> 00:23:47,427
But they're just names
that could work.

527
00:23:47,528 --> 00:23:49,230
-Right.
-Like Paul Hogan?

528
00:23:49,330 --> 00:23:51,431
Crocodile Dundee.

529
00:23:51,532 --> 00:23:53,935
Okay. No question.

530
00:23:54,035 --> 00:23:54,969
Bindi Irwin.

531
00:23:55,069 --> 00:23:57,872
[sighs] Now you're
just getting me excited.

532
00:23:57,972 --> 00:23:59,106
[chuckles]

533
00:23:59,206 --> 00:24:00,808
What about, uh...

534
00:24:00,908 --> 00:24:02,475
Yahoo Serious?

535
00:24:03,344 --> 00:24:04,812
-That's tough.
-Wouldn't work?

536
00:24:04,912 --> 00:24:06,814
-Paired? Maybe.
-Paired, yes.

537
00:24:06,914 --> 00:24:08,249
-With another celebrity?
-Yeah.

538
00:24:08,349 --> 00:24:11,085
-You'd have to get two people.
-One thing to consider.

539
00:24:11,185 --> 00:24:13,154
Were you able to confirm

540
00:24:13,254 --> 00:24:16,157
that you'd be able to have
kangaroos in the movie?

541
00:24:17,391 --> 00:24:18,359
Um...

542
00:24:18,458 --> 00:24:21,262
Yeah. We can
probably manage that.

543
00:24:21,362 --> 00:24:23,931
-Ooh! Koalas!
-Yeah.

544
00:24:24,031 --> 00:24:25,465
That's a must.

545
00:24:25,566 --> 00:24:26,968
-That's easier.
-Okay.

546
00:24:27,068 --> 00:24:28,569
They all have chlamydia.

547
00:24:28,669 --> 00:24:30,171
-I saw that on TikTok.
-Every single...?

548
00:24:30,271 --> 00:24:34,241
-That koalas have chlamydia.
-And every Australian as well.

549
00:24:34,342 --> 00:24:37,078
[laughs] That's perfect.

550
00:24:37,178 --> 00:24:38,879
That's good.

551
00:24:38,980 --> 00:24:41,048
[laughs]

552
00:24:42,616 --> 00:24:44,018
[Anthony, VO]
<i>So, now that we have a shortlist</i>

553
00:24:44,118 --> 00:24:47,021
<i>I need to fully master</i>
<i>The Asylum way.</i>

554
00:24:47,121 --> 00:24:49,457
So how many Asylum films
have you seen?

555
00:24:49,557 --> 00:24:51,425
[blows air] Like 25 to 30.

556
00:24:51,525 --> 00:24:53,027
-Maybe 40.
-That's disappointing.

557
00:24:53,127 --> 00:24:55,429
You have to see
at least 100 to 200

558
00:24:55,529 --> 00:24:57,098
before you call yourself a fan.

559
00:24:57,198 --> 00:25:00,234
Let's go all the way down here
to, like, the Syfy days.

560
00:25:00,334 --> 00:25:02,503
'Road Wars'.

561
00:25:02,603 --> 00:25:04,538
I haven't seen that
but I've seen 'Mad Max'.

562
00:25:04,638 --> 00:25:06,173
-I love that.
-Ha!

563
00:25:06,273 --> 00:25:11,278
Who is it that's driving the car
that you drove five years ago?!

564
00:25:11,379 --> 00:25:13,147
I don't know!

565
00:25:13,247 --> 00:25:14,782
LIAR!

566
00:25:15,583 --> 00:25:16,684
With Syfy

567
00:25:16,784 --> 00:25:18,452
we did zombie films

568
00:25:18,552 --> 00:25:19,820
I haven't seen that one.

569
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:21,489
See, you should probably
take that with you.

570
00:25:21,589 --> 00:25:24,692
You have a lot
of research to do.

571
00:25:24,792 --> 00:25:25,726
No.

572
00:25:25,826 --> 00:25:27,061
No!

573
00:25:28,262 --> 00:25:29,730
[screams]

574
00:25:31,966 --> 00:25:34,835
More infamously,
have you seen 'Shark Waters'?

575
00:25:36,337 --> 00:25:38,239
Move!

576
00:25:39,940 --> 00:25:41,075
[screams]

577
00:25:41,175 --> 00:25:44,078
That will be the
start of your homework.

578
00:25:44,178 --> 00:25:45,813
[laughs]

579
00:25:46,914 --> 00:25:48,349
[Anthony, VO]
<i>It's clear The Asylum</i>

580
00:25:48,449 --> 00:25:49,750
<i>have a specific set of rules.</i>

581
00:25:49,850 --> 00:25:52,853
<i>They all feature</i>
<i>a vaguely familiar star,</i>

582
00:25:52,953 --> 00:25:54,422
<i>the occasional use of CGI,</i>

583
00:25:54,523 --> 00:25:57,158
<i>tedious monologues,</i>
<i>ridiculous plots,</i>

584
00:25:57,258 --> 00:25:58,559
<i>cheesy action,</i>

585
00:25:58,659 --> 00:26:01,095
<i>and, thankfully,</i>
<i>they're all under 90 minutes.</i>

586
00:26:01,530 --> 00:26:05,733
<i>These movies all feel the same.</i>
<i>But I do see the potential.</i>

587
00:26:05,833 --> 00:26:08,369
<i>If I master the formula</i>

588
00:26:08,469 --> 00:26:09,837
<i>and twist it just enough</i>

589
00:26:09,937 --> 00:26:13,207
<i>I can turn the familiar</i>
<i>into the unforgettable.</i>

590
00:26:13,307 --> 00:26:15,209
What films have
you written for The Asylum?

591
00:26:15,309 --> 00:26:16,710
Well, I had a string

592
00:26:16,811 --> 00:26:18,779
of meteor disaster movies
that I'd written.

593
00:26:18,879 --> 00:26:21,148
One was 'Collision Earth'.

594
00:26:23,584 --> 00:26:24,952
'Meteor Moon'.

595
00:26:25,052 --> 00:26:28,622
The Moon gets crashed into,
that turns into a meteor.

596
00:26:28,722 --> 00:26:29,924
It's gonna crash into the Earth.

597
00:26:30,024 --> 00:26:31,625
'Moon Crash'.

598
00:26:31,725 --> 00:26:34,395
Similar to 'Meteor Moon'.

599
00:26:34,495 --> 00:26:36,730
It's all mostly marketing

600
00:26:36,831 --> 00:26:38,899
that really
brings our viewers in.

601
00:26:38,999 --> 00:26:40,668
So it's gotta
have a catchy title

602
00:26:40,768 --> 00:26:42,069
and catchy artwork.

603
00:26:42,169 --> 00:26:44,705
Nobody's really looking
for an Asylum title.

604
00:26:44,805 --> 00:26:47,141
They're sort of
scrolling and scrolling

605
00:26:47,241 --> 00:26:48,776
and then it's, like,
Great! 'The Land Before Time'.

606
00:26:48,876 --> 00:26:50,945
You know what I mean?
Let's watch this. Sure.

607
00:26:51,045 --> 00:26:54,081
One of yours
is called 'Methgator', right?

608
00:26:54,181 --> 00:26:55,116
That's right, yeah.

609
00:26:55,216 --> 00:26:56,585
And what was that about?

610
00:26:56,684 --> 00:26:58,486
It's in the title,
thank goodness.

611
00:26:58,587 --> 00:26:59,987
There's no subtlety.

612
00:27:00,087 --> 00:27:03,324
Alligator eats a bunch of meth,
slaughters a bunch of people.

613
00:27:03,424 --> 00:27:05,459
I'm sorry for the spoilers.

614
00:27:05,560 --> 00:27:08,362
A lot of people
can do big-budget films.

615
00:27:08,462 --> 00:27:11,732
Easy. Anytime there's a problem
you throw a little bit of money.

616
00:27:11,832 --> 00:27:15,236
Not anybody can
produce an Asylum film.

617
00:27:15,336 --> 00:27:16,971
And succeed. And do it well.

618
00:27:17,071 --> 00:27:19,073
There's a very
high whiplash factor.

619
00:27:19,173 --> 00:27:21,543
So there's a lot of
being like on top of the world

620
00:27:21,642 --> 00:27:23,043
and then the next morning
it all goes to shit.

621
00:27:23,144 --> 00:27:25,312
And then you're really mad
and suicidal.

622
00:27:25,412 --> 00:27:27,047
And then something good happens

623
00:27:27,148 --> 00:27:28,583
and then you're feeling
really good about that.

624
00:27:28,682 --> 00:27:29,717
Then you're, like,
wait a minute.

625
00:27:29,817 --> 00:27:31,118
Am I being naive
feeling good about this?

626
00:27:31,218 --> 00:27:33,220
And then turns out you are,
'coz something horrible happens.

627
00:27:33,320 --> 00:27:36,423
What's the balance between
art and commerce at The Asylum?

628
00:27:36,525 --> 00:27:39,994
[laughs] Uh...

629
00:27:40,094 --> 00:27:43,063
It's subject
to time constraints,

630
00:27:43,164 --> 00:27:44,165
budget constraints

631
00:27:44,265 --> 00:27:46,233
the notes we're getting
from upstairs.

632
00:27:46,333 --> 00:27:48,702
Is it my movie? Not really.
You know what I mean?

633
00:27:48,802 --> 00:27:50,437
You just have to accept that.

634
00:27:50,539 --> 00:27:52,006
It's commerce.

635
00:27:52,106 --> 00:27:54,208
If there is artistry involved

636
00:27:54,308 --> 00:27:57,044
there's definitely
the visual effects department.

637
00:27:58,846 --> 00:28:01,315
[man]
We've got five people on staff

638
00:28:01,415 --> 00:28:02,883
and we have 10 freelancers.

639
00:28:02,983 --> 00:28:05,587
So, basically
it's all hands on deck

640
00:28:05,686 --> 00:28:07,054
and we do what we have to.

641
00:28:07,154 --> 00:28:10,391
And if you watch,
you will see what we do.

642
00:28:10,491 --> 00:28:13,327
So, there's the shark. Oh!
It gets attacked by a dolphin.

643
00:28:13,427 --> 00:28:14,995
So, there's the animation.

644
00:28:15,095 --> 00:28:16,764
There's the wire frame
of the dolphin and the shark.

645
00:28:16,864 --> 00:28:19,099
This is what we do
for every shot.

646
00:28:19,200 --> 00:28:21,936
When a director
doesn't know the difference

647
00:28:22,036 --> 00:28:23,470
between better and different

648
00:28:23,572 --> 00:28:25,139
we get into trouble.

649
00:28:25,239 --> 00:28:27,808
"What if it did this?"
Well, that shot's done.

650
00:28:27,908 --> 00:28:29,410
"Yeah,
but what if it did this?"

651
00:28:29,511 --> 00:28:31,445
Oh, my God. Pick your battles.

652
00:28:31,546 --> 00:28:35,684
You guys make your movie
in seven days for seven dollars.

653
00:28:35,783 --> 00:28:38,485
I have to do
the equivalent of that in post.

654
00:28:38,587 --> 00:28:40,487
"But my vision, my art!"

655
00:28:40,589 --> 00:28:42,356
I got three weeks.

656
00:28:42,456 --> 00:28:44,925
I know you wanna make
your artsy fartsy film.

657
00:28:45,025 --> 00:28:48,062
We don't have
the time and budget for it.

658
00:28:48,162 --> 00:28:49,763
[cat meows]

659
00:28:49,863 --> 00:28:53,267
So, our door is always open.
My phone's never off.

660
00:28:53,367 --> 00:28:54,802
Thank you for checking.

661
00:28:56,003 --> 00:28:58,973
<i>Even if this movie</i>
<i>isn't exactly my dream project</i>

662
00:28:59,073 --> 00:29:01,108
<i>it can put me on the right path.</i>

663
00:29:01,208 --> 00:29:02,376
<i>If I nail it,</i>

664
00:29:02,476 --> 00:29:06,113
<i>I could be the next big thing</i>
<i>out of The Asylum.</i>

665
00:29:06,213 --> 00:29:08,449
<i>That's not something</i>
<i>to take on lightly.</i>

666
00:29:08,550 --> 00:29:10,918
You guys got everything you
need? You need anything else?

667
00:29:11,018 --> 00:29:13,254
Do you have any final
words of advice or warning?

668
00:29:13,354 --> 00:29:15,122
Don't do it. Run. Um...

669
00:29:15,222 --> 00:29:16,857
It'll be over
before you know it.

670
00:29:16,957 --> 00:29:18,926
Try and enjoy it while you can.

671
00:29:19,026 --> 00:29:20,861
It's gonna be very stressful.

672
00:29:20,961 --> 00:29:22,229
Go with the flow.

673
00:29:22,329 --> 00:29:24,666
Just make sure
the actors know their lines.

674
00:29:24,765 --> 00:29:29,336
The more authentic things look,
the better.

675
00:29:29,436 --> 00:29:30,904
Alright.

676
00:29:31,005 --> 00:29:32,873
-Thanks again.
-Good travels. Alright?

677
00:29:32,973 --> 00:29:34,008
Thanks for the opportunity.

678
00:29:34,108 --> 00:29:35,677
I look forward
to seeing the dailies

679
00:29:35,776 --> 00:29:38,212
and, uh...

680
00:29:38,312 --> 00:29:39,413
Good luck!

681
00:29:39,514 --> 00:29:42,416
[upbeat music]

682
00:30:03,103 --> 00:30:06,407
I've been back a week already
and still don't have a script.

683
00:30:06,508 --> 00:30:08,777
There's not really much
I can do without the script.

684
00:30:08,876 --> 00:30:11,312
So, I can't get cast,
I can't get crew.

685
00:30:11,412 --> 00:30:13,213
I can't get locations.

686
00:30:14,582 --> 00:30:16,850
I assume it's all happening.

687
00:30:16,950 --> 00:30:18,419
[laughs]
I haven't heard otherwise.

688
00:30:18,520 --> 00:30:22,356
But it's starting to feel like
LA was some weird fever dream...

689
00:30:24,659 --> 00:30:25,893
Some prank.

690
00:30:25,993 --> 00:30:29,330
Like, you'd think, if you're
making a movie in six days

691
00:30:29,430 --> 00:30:32,600
you'd give the filmmakers
more time to prepare.

692
00:30:32,701 --> 00:30:33,768
[laughs]

693
00:30:33,867 --> 00:30:36,170
But they don't.

694
00:30:36,270 --> 00:30:39,406
[phone rings]

695
00:30:40,441 --> 00:30:42,276
[phone rings]

696
00:30:42,376 --> 00:30:46,313
-[Brendan] <i>Hello, Anthony.</i>
-Hey, how are you doin'?

697
00:30:46,413 --> 00:30:49,818
<i>I'm good. Just, you know,</i>
<i>just working over this budget.</i>

698
00:30:49,917 --> 00:30:51,919
<i>-How are you doin'?</i>
-Yeah, not too bad.

699
00:30:52,019 --> 00:30:53,954
Do you know when
the script's coming through?

700
00:30:54,054 --> 00:30:56,791
<i>That is a great question.</i>

701
00:30:56,890 --> 00:30:58,693
<i>You know, it is delayed.</i>

702
00:30:58,793 --> 00:31:02,564
<i>They are doing another pass</i>
<i>with more notes and stuff.</i>

703
00:31:02,664 --> 00:31:04,532
<i>But you have the one-pager.</i>

704
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:08,703
<i>The film's always</i>
<i>gonna stick to the one-pager.</i>

705
00:31:08,803 --> 00:31:10,871
-Okay.
<i>-So you're not walking blind.</i>

706
00:31:10,971 --> 00:31:14,174
<i>You're just walking...</i>
<i>slightly blind.</i>

707
00:31:14,975 --> 00:31:16,644
Alright. Thanks for that.

708
00:31:16,745 --> 00:31:18,813
<i>Of course!</i>

709
00:31:18,912 --> 00:31:20,715
Chat soon. Catch ya.

710
00:31:20,815 --> 00:31:22,349
<i>Bye!</i>

711
00:31:26,588 --> 00:31:28,556
[Anthony, VO]
<i>No-one's gonna take me seriously</i>

712
00:31:28,656 --> 00:31:29,990
<i>without a script.</i>

713
00:31:30,090 --> 00:31:31,492
<i>But I can't keep waiting for it.</i>

714
00:31:31,593 --> 00:31:33,695
"Have you ever dreamed of
working on a Hollywood movie?

715
00:31:33,795 --> 00:31:34,928
Well, now's your chance."

716
00:31:35,028 --> 00:31:37,766
<i>Part of the deal</i>
<i>is that I find a local producer,</i>

717
00:31:37,866 --> 00:31:39,701
<i>someone who can</i>
<i>do all the boring stuff</i>

718
00:31:39,801 --> 00:31:41,235
<i>like insurance and contracting.</i>

719
00:31:41,335 --> 00:31:43,237
"I haven't seen a script yet.
We shoot in a few weeks.

720
00:31:43,337 --> 00:31:44,739
Production will take six days."

721
00:31:44,839 --> 00:31:48,610
<i>But mostly someone else</i>
<i>who believes in this movie.</i>

722
00:31:49,677 --> 00:31:51,078
I don't know where to start.

723
00:31:51,178 --> 00:31:53,347
But there's no script,
no budget.

724
00:31:53,447 --> 00:31:55,382
We're kind of starting
from square one here.

725
00:31:55,482 --> 00:31:58,986
You mentioned you
may have read a one-pager?

726
00:31:59,086 --> 00:32:01,790
Yeah. I can't make sense of it.

727
00:32:01,890 --> 00:32:03,056
Oh.

728
00:32:03,157 --> 00:32:06,360
And it makes less sense
with every readthrough.

729
00:32:08,095 --> 00:32:09,697
There's someone
with a dying mother.

730
00:32:09,798 --> 00:32:12,099
-What?
-[laughs]

731
00:32:12,734 --> 00:32:16,270
Someone's mom's in hospital,
they're trying to get back to...

732
00:32:16,370 --> 00:32:18,840
Oh, okay.
It's pretty jam-packed.

733
00:32:18,939 --> 00:32:20,307
Does this worry you?

734
00:32:20,407 --> 00:32:23,611
The filmmaker in me... [laughs]

735
00:32:23,711 --> 00:32:25,012
knows that this is a bad idea.

736
00:32:25,112 --> 00:32:26,947
It'll be a hellish week

737
00:32:27,047 --> 00:32:30,350
and we'll come out of this
as ragged husks.

738
00:32:31,786 --> 00:32:33,588
[both laugh]

739
00:32:33,688 --> 00:32:35,389
This is wild.

740
00:32:35,489 --> 00:32:37,224
-Yep.
-Mmm.

741
00:32:37,324 --> 00:32:38,827
I think the script
and the budget

742
00:32:38,927 --> 00:32:42,129
will be the next question,
really.

743
00:32:43,297 --> 00:32:44,933
[Anthony, VO]
<i>I'm trying to play it cool</i>

744
00:32:45,032 --> 00:32:47,267
<i>and not let on</i>
<i>how desperate I am.</i>

745
00:32:47,367 --> 00:32:51,038
<i>But without a local production</i>
<i>company to oversee the shoot</i>

746
00:32:51,138 --> 00:32:54,241
<i>The Asylum</i>
<i>will pull the plug on me.</i>

747
00:32:57,110 --> 00:32:58,746
[clears throat]

748
00:32:58,847 --> 00:33:01,415
"Exterior. Ocean, day.

749
00:33:01,516 --> 00:33:04,953
A US Navy barge moves
through the vast Bering Sea

750
00:33:05,052 --> 00:33:07,287
with no land
or other sign of life in sight."

751
00:33:07,387 --> 00:33:09,657
<i>Finally. The script came in.</i>

752
00:33:09,757 --> 00:33:12,527
<i>It feels... ambitious.</i>

753
00:33:12,627 --> 00:33:13,628
I'm nine pages in.

754
00:33:13,728 --> 00:33:14,963
There's been, like...

755
00:33:15,062 --> 00:33:18,800
explosions, dinosaurs,
submarines

756
00:33:18,900 --> 00:33:21,368
about six characters, wet suits

757
00:33:21,468 --> 00:33:23,370
underwater shots.

758
00:33:23,470 --> 00:33:24,873
[laughs]

759
00:33:24,973 --> 00:33:26,908
"Maybe this is hell.

760
00:33:27,007 --> 00:33:28,308
Maybe we already died.

761
00:33:28,408 --> 00:33:30,477
That's why Ahm says
we can't get off this island.

762
00:33:30,578 --> 00:33:33,748
It'll be like this for eternity.
I'll never see my mom again."

763
00:33:35,750 --> 00:33:38,218
There's 34 locations.

764
00:33:38,318 --> 00:33:40,387
We're gonna have
to find a forest and-or woods

765
00:33:40,487 --> 00:33:42,991
that can play as every location.

766
00:33:43,090 --> 00:33:45,459
Otherwise this is impossible.

767
00:33:45,560 --> 00:33:50,932
Welcome to the land that...
no-one knew was there.

768
00:33:51,031 --> 00:33:53,233
There's a lot we can do here.

769
00:33:53,333 --> 00:33:55,469
Getting pulled down
by the T-rex?

770
00:33:55,570 --> 00:33:58,171
Yeah, totally. That
looks like a sheer cliff face.

771
00:33:58,272 --> 00:34:00,173
[laughs] It's great.

772
00:34:00,274 --> 00:34:02,610
The magic of cinema.

773
00:34:02,710 --> 00:34:04,411
Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

774
00:34:04,512 --> 00:34:06,581
You know.

775
00:34:06,681 --> 00:34:08,081
It's not my call but...

776
00:34:08,181 --> 00:34:10,183
It's relatively safe.

777
00:34:11,653 --> 00:34:12,520
Relatively.

778
00:34:12,620 --> 00:34:15,690
Whoa!
Alright, come up here, Aaron.

779
00:34:15,790 --> 00:34:17,592
Have a little geez, mate.

780
00:34:20,127 --> 00:34:23,330
Look at this.
From here, where you are, look.

781
00:34:23,430 --> 00:34:25,700
I'm gonna go deeper
into the cave.

782
00:34:25,800 --> 00:34:26,868
Ha-ha! Oh, yeah!

783
00:34:26,968 --> 00:34:29,202
Oh, man. And I'm gonna...

784
00:34:29,303 --> 00:34:31,338
And if you shoot
even from there...

785
00:34:31,438 --> 00:34:34,141
I'm gonna go deeper
into the cave now, Anthony.

786
00:34:34,241 --> 00:34:38,178
If I don't come back...
call my mom.

787
00:34:38,980 --> 00:34:41,883
Ohh! Hello! Hello!

788
00:34:41,983 --> 00:34:43,851
-Right?
-Yeah.

789
00:34:43,952 --> 00:34:45,753
You know, wide shot down here.

790
00:34:45,853 --> 00:34:47,922
Whoa, the wind.

791
00:34:48,656 --> 00:34:51,358
<i>I think I found the perfect</i>
<i>director of photography.</i>

792
00:34:51,458 --> 00:34:54,161
Even here's a little shot.
Do you think? The rocks.

793
00:34:54,261 --> 00:34:55,797
<i>And it seems like</i>

794
00:34:55,897 --> 00:34:59,734
<i>he really understands</i>
<i>my vision for this film.</i>

795
00:34:59,834 --> 00:35:01,335
This is amazing.

796
00:35:01,435 --> 00:35:05,907
Yeah.
You can't get that in Burbank.

797
00:35:06,007 --> 00:35:08,108
<i>But without Tom on board</i>

798
00:35:08,208 --> 00:35:11,546
<i>Aaron's hesitant</i>
<i>to make this official.</i>

799
00:35:12,179 --> 00:35:15,717
<i>Thankfully,</i>
<i>negotiations are going great.</i>

800
00:35:15,817 --> 00:35:16,884
Just silly.

801
00:35:16,985 --> 00:35:18,118
<i>Tom's on the hook.</i>

802
00:35:18,218 --> 00:35:21,154
Brendan just sent an email,
by the way.

803
00:35:21,254 --> 00:35:22,757
What did he say?

804
00:35:22,857 --> 00:35:24,191
Changes.

805
00:35:24,291 --> 00:35:25,425
Second AD - one prep day.

806
00:35:25,526 --> 00:35:27,829
PA - one prep day, one wrap day.

807
00:35:27,929 --> 00:35:29,229
DP - one prep day.

808
00:35:29,329 --> 00:35:31,799
-That's fucking crazy.
-Nah, nah, nah.

809
00:35:31,899 --> 00:35:33,534
That's not good enough.

810
00:35:34,636 --> 00:35:38,039
<i>But this is his first real look</i>
<i>at the budget.</i>

811
00:35:38,138 --> 00:35:39,306
<i>And he's not amused.</i>

812
00:35:39,406 --> 00:35:41,776
I don't mean to alarm anyone

813
00:35:41,876 --> 00:35:44,646
but that's not doable.

814
00:35:44,746 --> 00:35:46,914
Not even funny ha-ha.

815
00:35:47,015 --> 00:35:48,783
-Like, that's...
-Impossible.

816
00:35:48,883 --> 00:35:50,518
That's just...

817
00:35:52,152 --> 00:35:54,689
I'd, like... We can't do it.

818
00:35:54,789 --> 00:35:56,323
Like, actually.

819
00:35:56,423 --> 00:35:58,191
What are the expectations?

820
00:35:58,291 --> 00:35:59,560
I want a product

821
00:35:59,661 --> 00:36:02,063
but I'm giving you
no money and no time.

822
00:36:02,162 --> 00:36:03,665
Make the thing.

823
00:36:05,265 --> 00:36:07,935
[somber music plays]

824
00:36:08,036 --> 00:36:09,937
Do you have a Plan B?

825
00:36:11,639 --> 00:36:13,641
[laughs]

826
00:36:13,741 --> 00:36:15,308
Not really.

827
00:36:21,115 --> 00:36:22,083
Sorry.

828
00:36:22,182 --> 00:36:23,885
I'm so sorry.

829
00:36:27,121 --> 00:36:29,023
And mostly I'm just upset
for you.

830
00:36:29,123 --> 00:36:31,425
'Coz this is really important.

831
00:36:39,399 --> 00:36:40,735
It's like everything I made
up to this

832
00:36:40,835 --> 00:36:42,369
has just been
friends getting together

833
00:36:42,469 --> 00:36:44,839
and making something for the
love of just making something.

834
00:36:44,939 --> 00:36:47,374
And now it's, like, we
have to worry about insurance

835
00:36:47,474 --> 00:36:48,776
and contracts and cast

836
00:36:48,876 --> 00:36:52,345
and all these fuckin' things
that you just never think about.

837
00:36:52,446 --> 00:36:55,616
So without a producer
we don't have the movie.

838
00:36:55,717 --> 00:36:57,852
I don't know
what the fuck to do.

839
00:37:02,489 --> 00:37:06,127
<i>This just went</i>
<i>from difficult to impossible.</i>

840
00:37:06,226 --> 00:37:08,196
<i>With Tom out of the picture</i>

841
00:37:08,295 --> 00:37:10,263
<i>and no production company</i>
<i>backing me</i>

842
00:37:10,363 --> 00:37:13,901
<i>I'm not holding up</i>
<i>my end of the bargain.</i>

843
00:37:14,001 --> 00:37:15,435
Yeah.

844
00:37:15,536 --> 00:37:18,005
<i>I'm scared to tell The Asylum.</i>

845
00:37:18,106 --> 00:37:20,942
No, he's not doing it.

846
00:37:22,476 --> 00:37:25,913
<i>I just can't let them</i>
<i>take this film away from me.</i>

847
00:37:27,380 --> 00:37:30,718
<i>They decided</i>
<i>there's only one solution left..</i>

848
00:37:38,192 --> 00:37:40,762
<i>They're sending over</i>
<i>their enforcer.</i>

849
00:37:46,934 --> 00:37:48,870
How are you doin'?

850
00:37:48,970 --> 00:37:50,303
A little tired.

851
00:37:50,403 --> 00:37:53,007
So this is
the Adelaide airport?

852
00:37:53,107 --> 00:37:54,776
I love that.

853
00:37:54,876 --> 00:37:56,644
You might get
a glimpse of the city.

854
00:37:56,744 --> 00:37:59,147
[sighs]
I think I'm nervous about

855
00:37:59,247 --> 00:38:00,782
getting everything together
in time.

856
00:38:00,882 --> 00:38:01,949
'Coz it's really...

857
00:38:02,049 --> 00:38:03,251
Today's Wednesday.

858
00:38:03,350 --> 00:38:05,052
We were planning
to shoot Tuesday.

859
00:38:05,153 --> 00:38:07,822
And nothing's really locked yet.

860
00:38:07,922 --> 00:38:09,757
-Yep.
-Is this normal?

861
00:38:09,857 --> 00:38:11,726
Very much.

862
00:38:11,826 --> 00:38:14,095
I mean,
why do you keep doing it?

863
00:38:14,195 --> 00:38:16,329
Because it's addicting.

864
00:38:17,330 --> 00:38:18,766
You know, what, last week

865
00:38:18,866 --> 00:38:21,501
I was doing,
like, a fantasy medieval movie.

866
00:38:21,602 --> 00:38:23,604
And now I'm doing,
like, a modern

867
00:38:23,704 --> 00:38:25,907
jungle island adventure film.

868
00:38:26,007 --> 00:38:29,911
I have an ulcer from the stress
in the back of my throat.

869
00:38:30,011 --> 00:38:32,079
Oh, my God.

870
00:38:32,180 --> 00:38:33,781
Welcome to Asylum.

871
00:38:33,881 --> 00:38:35,283
[laughs]

872
00:38:35,382 --> 00:38:37,585
<i>My movie lives</i>
<i>to fight another day.</i>

873
00:38:37,685 --> 00:38:40,320
It's, like,
this little number here

874
00:38:40,420 --> 00:38:42,790
has to go...

875
00:38:42,890 --> 00:38:44,525
to that.

876
00:38:44,625 --> 00:38:47,094
<i>Now I don't need to find</i>
<i>a local production company.</i>

877
00:38:47,195 --> 00:38:50,531
<i>Because I basically</i>
<i>have The Asylum here with me.</i>

878
00:38:50,631 --> 00:38:52,166
You can just chop
here, here, here,

879
00:38:52,266 --> 00:38:54,735
like, all three of these
don't need 'em.

880
00:38:54,836 --> 00:38:58,072
<i>Brendan knows how</i>
<i>to get the numbers working.</i>

881
00:38:58,172 --> 00:38:59,707
<i>The Asylum way.</i>

882
00:38:59,807 --> 00:39:01,676
I've literally seen
the impossible happen.

883
00:39:01,776 --> 00:39:03,177
I've made
the impossible happen.

884
00:39:03,277 --> 00:39:04,679
So for me,
I'm just like, "It's fine."

885
00:39:04,779 --> 00:39:06,881
But it's just like
explaining to someone else

886
00:39:06,981 --> 00:39:09,416
who's just not used to that,
you know....

887
00:39:09,517 --> 00:39:11,451
You can do this. It's possible.

888
00:39:11,552 --> 00:39:13,988
Mentally not easy, but...
[laughs]

889
00:39:14,088 --> 00:39:16,356
It's how we do it.

890
00:39:16,456 --> 00:39:18,025
[hopeful music]

891
00:39:18,125 --> 00:39:20,427
<i>Thankfully, there</i>
<i>was still room in the budget</i>

892
00:39:20,528 --> 00:39:21,829
<i>for some crew.</i>

893
00:39:21,929 --> 00:39:24,732
I'm here with Brendan, who's
a producer from The Asylum

894
00:39:24,832 --> 00:39:26,767
talking about potential crew
for our shoot.

895
00:39:26,868 --> 00:39:28,769
<i>And I was hoping</i>
<i>a bit of LA flair</i>

896
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:30,838
<i>would help get</i>
<i>the local industry behind me.</i>

897
00:39:30,938 --> 00:39:31,839
Yeah, okay.

898
00:39:31,939 --> 00:39:33,274
If you wouldn't mind
sending me his details,

899
00:39:33,373 --> 00:39:34,709
I can give him a buzz.

900
00:39:34,809 --> 00:39:37,545
<i>We are in that water the second</i>
<i>Bradley gives us the signal.</i>

901
00:39:37,645 --> 00:39:40,514
<i>Then boom! The Russians go down</i>
<i>with this fucking barge!</i>

902
00:39:40,615 --> 00:39:42,283
-Yep.
-Yeah, he's good.

903
00:39:42,382 --> 00:39:44,819
I don't foresee a 40-page day
on this show

904
00:39:44,919 --> 00:39:47,889
but 20- to 30-page day
could happen at least once.

905
00:39:47,989 --> 00:39:50,423
Um... A bit of experience
would be good.

906
00:39:50,524 --> 00:39:52,193
No answer.

907
00:39:52,293 --> 00:39:54,595
<i>The only way we will</i>
<i>ever be safe is if we kill it.</i>

908
00:39:54,695 --> 00:39:56,764
<i>We have to kill the T-rex.</i>

909
00:39:56,864 --> 00:39:58,366
She's got the Asylum look,
you know?

910
00:39:58,465 --> 00:40:00,201
And she's sold this scene,
I think.

911
00:40:00,301 --> 00:40:01,802
-I would put her first.
-Yeah.

912
00:40:01,903 --> 00:40:05,506
I just need someone
who has insurance

913
00:40:05,606 --> 00:40:07,975
and who I can pay
to pay the cast and crew.

914
00:40:08,075 --> 00:40:09,810
Besides acting,

915
00:40:09,911 --> 00:40:12,713
is that something
you'd be interested in?

916
00:40:12,813 --> 00:40:13,981
[laughs]

917
00:40:14,081 --> 00:40:16,217
I really want Josh Davies
because he's my best friend.

918
00:40:16,317 --> 00:40:17,919
<i>Ha-ha.</i>

919
00:40:18,019 --> 00:40:20,254
<i>Here we go. Success.</i>

920
00:40:20,354 --> 00:40:21,656
[woman, on phone]
<i>I messaged Anthony,</i>

921
00:40:21,756 --> 00:40:23,157
<i>said I was keen</i>
<i>to script supervise.</i>

922
00:40:23,257 --> 00:40:24,358
Well, would you want
to jump back

923
00:40:24,457 --> 00:40:25,626
into firsting for a show?

924
00:40:25,726 --> 00:40:27,695
I'm not sure.
Do you have a script supervisor?

925
00:40:27,795 --> 00:40:30,031
[laughs] They don't
even have that in the budget.

926
00:40:30,131 --> 00:40:32,667
I feel like things are actually
moving now that you're here.

927
00:40:32,767 --> 00:40:35,636
Yeah, as they should.
That's why I'm here.

928
00:40:35,736 --> 00:40:38,406
<i>Order 66 now! Go! Go!</i>

929
00:40:38,506 --> 00:40:40,074
[Anthony, VO]
<i>I achieved more with Brendan</i>

930
00:40:40,174 --> 00:40:41,676
<i>in the last two days</i>

931
00:40:41,776 --> 00:40:43,678
<i>than I did</i>
<i>in the last two weeks.</i>

932
00:40:43,778 --> 00:40:45,246
[man, on phone]
<i>This is a perfect scenario.</i>

933
00:40:45,346 --> 00:40:46,847
Great. Awesome.

934
00:40:46,948 --> 00:40:47,982
-Cool.
-Cool.

935
00:40:48,082 --> 00:40:49,116
Welcome to the show.

936
00:40:49,216 --> 00:40:50,584
-Thanks, Ben.
-Bye.

937
00:40:50,685 --> 00:40:52,820
It's great. All right, done.
All right, let's get drinks.

938
00:40:52,920 --> 00:40:55,423
Yeah.
Let's make this fucking movie.

939
00:40:59,994 --> 00:41:03,264
<i>The one thing we can't land</i>
<i>is an Australian name actor.</i>

940
00:41:03,364 --> 00:41:07,802
Budget-wise, it's 300
Australian a day for 12 hours.

941
00:41:07,902 --> 00:41:10,805
I can give you guys
about an hour

942
00:41:10,905 --> 00:41:12,640
but then I have to
move on to somebody else.

943
00:41:12,740 --> 00:41:15,810
<i>For some reason every</i>
<i>Aussie with a profile is busy</i>

944
00:41:15,910 --> 00:41:17,378
<i>the moment they hear from us.</i>

945
00:41:17,477 --> 00:41:18,546
Okay.

946
00:41:18,646 --> 00:41:20,614
<i>So we're</i>
<i>forging on without one.</i>

947
00:41:20,715 --> 00:41:22,016
<i>It's a shame.</i>

948
00:41:22,116 --> 00:41:24,484
<i>I was really looking forward</i>
<i>to working with a star.</i>

949
00:41:24,585 --> 00:41:26,587
<i>And now I'm gonna have to</i>

950
00:41:26,687 --> 00:41:28,289
<i>get some pretty</i>
<i>stellar performances</i>

951
