fucking two people on overtime.

1870
01:19:45,886 --> 01:19:47,555
And action.

1871
01:19:47,656 --> 01:19:50,958
Admiral,
we're maxed out at 15 knots,

1872
01:19:51,058 --> 01:19:54,128
about 10 nautical miles
from the Australian barge.

1873
01:19:54,228 --> 01:19:56,463
The Swiss haven't reported
anything out of the ordinary,

1874
01:19:56,564 --> 01:19:58,932
but I'll update you if they do.

1875
01:19:59,033 --> 01:20:00,067
[Michael Pare]
The sooner we get there

1876
01:20:00,167 --> 01:20:01,335
the sooner
we'll know what the heck...

1877
01:20:01,435 --> 01:20:03,437
<i>Then it clicked</i>
<i>why I'm actually doing this.</i>

1878
01:20:03,538 --> 01:20:07,141
Signs of life, rescue boat,
items left behind.

1879
01:20:07,241 --> 01:20:09,577
<i>It isn't to fix any problems.</i>

1880
01:20:09,678 --> 01:20:13,013
<i>They just need me to shoehorn</i>
<i>a name actor into this movie.</i>

1881
01:20:13,113 --> 01:20:14,982
<i>It's a contractual obligation.</i>

1882
01:20:15,082 --> 01:20:16,685
Yes or no?
Go again, right?

1883
01:20:16,785 --> 01:20:18,085
-Yes or no?
-I think we're good.

1884
01:20:18,185 --> 01:20:20,187
-Yeah, I think it's okay.
-You like it, we like it?

1885
01:20:20,287 --> 01:20:22,691
-Okay, moving on.
-Let's lower camera.

1886
01:20:22,791 --> 01:20:25,660
<i>With the growing prospect</i>
<i>of going over time and budget</i>

1887
01:20:25,760 --> 01:20:29,496
<i>and that I'm making a ridiculous</i>
<i>film even more confusing,</i>

1888
01:20:29,597 --> 01:20:33,000
<i>I realized this isn't about me.</i>

1889
01:20:35,469 --> 01:20:38,038
<i>I'm just a cog</i>
<i>in the Asylum machine.</i>

1890
01:20:38,138 --> 01:20:41,208
Full speed ahead!
Get us out of here now!

1891
01:20:41,308 --> 01:20:44,612
<i>No-one will</i>
<i>remember who directed this film.</i>

1892
01:20:44,713 --> 01:20:48,148
Scene BD53A, take 2.

1893
01:20:48,248 --> 01:20:54,321
<i>I'm an Asylum director, and I'm</i>
<i>making a goddamn Asylum film.</i>

1894
01:20:54,421 --> 01:20:55,989
And action.

1895
01:20:57,826 --> 01:20:59,527
[Paul Bales]
People say to me all the time,

1896
01:20:59,627 --> 01:21:01,128
"Why don't you, you know,

1897
01:21:01,228 --> 01:21:03,063
just save up your money
and make a good movie?"

1898
01:21:03,163 --> 01:21:05,065
And you know, after I kill them,

1899
01:21:05,165 --> 01:21:08,235
I say, "No-one knows
what a good movie is."

1900
01:21:08,335 --> 01:21:10,705
And I think
that's very, very true.

1901
01:21:10,805 --> 01:21:13,240
Many, many
companies before ours

1902
01:21:13,340 --> 01:21:16,544
have bet the farm
on making a good movie

1903
01:21:16,644 --> 01:21:17,978
and lost everything.

1904
01:21:18,078 --> 01:21:21,348
And that's just not the model
that we pursue.

1905
01:21:23,785 --> 01:21:26,153
I don't read reviews anymore.

1906
01:21:26,253 --> 01:21:29,890
Some people online
are very bored and mean.

1907
01:21:29,990 --> 01:21:31,892
Didn't get enough love
from their mother.

1908
01:21:31,992 --> 01:21:36,063
But the most egregious failure
for an Asylum movie

1909
01:21:36,163 --> 01:21:40,434
is that it's dull and boring
the crap out of the audience.

1910
01:21:40,535 --> 01:21:45,673
If you make the best film now,
oh, my God!

1911
01:21:45,774 --> 01:21:46,940
[scoffs]

1912
01:21:47,040 --> 01:21:49,276
That's like winning an Oscar
on your first movie.

1913
01:21:49,376 --> 01:21:50,879
That's horrible.

1914
01:21:50,978 --> 01:21:52,680
You know,
where do you go from there?

1915
01:21:52,781 --> 01:21:54,682
Ugh! What a lousy existence.

1916
01:21:54,783 --> 01:21:56,551
You're done with this.

1917
01:21:58,152 --> 01:22:00,287
[Anthony, VO]
<i>I always thought I was destined</i>

1918
01:22:00,387 --> 01:22:02,089
<i>for a place among the greats.</i>

1919
01:22:02,189 --> 01:22:05,259
<i>I just had no idea</i>
<i>what that actually meant for me.</i>

1920
01:22:05,359 --> 01:22:06,594
<i>Until now.</i>

1921
01:22:08,095 --> 01:22:10,364
<i>...affects the speed of time.</i>

1922
01:22:10,464 --> 01:22:14,134
<i>Like a black hole.</i>
<i>Like, time dilation.</i>

1923
01:22:14,234 --> 01:22:16,136
<i>Maybe the real reason</i>
<i>I reached out to the Asylum</i>

1924
01:22:16,236 --> 01:22:18,038
<i>is because, deep down,</i>

1925
01:22:18,138 --> 01:22:20,340
<i>I knew this</i>
<i>is where I should be.</i>

1926
01:22:29,016 --> 01:22:30,852
<i>Everything I've ever done</i>

1927
01:22:30,951 --> 01:22:33,153
<i>has been building</i>
<i>towards this moment.</i>

1928
01:22:33,253 --> 01:22:36,290
In the land that time forgot!

1929
01:22:36,390 --> 01:22:38,225
[laughs]

1930
01:22:38,325 --> 01:22:40,628
[Brendan]
Anthony, how are we doing?

1931
01:22:40,728 --> 01:22:42,463
What's that? Last set up.

1932
01:22:42,564 --> 01:22:44,064
Last set up.

1933
01:22:44,164 --> 01:22:47,569
-Last shot.
-Last shot. Okay.

1934
01:22:50,204 --> 01:22:52,507
<i>Because when no-one else</i>
<i>was willing to give me a shot,</i>

1935
01:22:52,607 --> 01:22:54,475
<i>the Asylum did.</i>

1936
01:22:55,309 --> 01:22:57,311
<i>Not because they believed in me,</i>

1937
01:22:57,411 --> 01:23:00,180
<i>just that they</i>
<i>needed someone to do it.</i>

1938
01:23:00,280 --> 01:23:03,317
<i>My instruments, they're</i>
<i>all going off the charts.</i>

1939
01:23:03,417 --> 01:23:04,752
Cut.

1940
01:23:04,853 --> 01:23:06,921
How long do we have?

1941
01:23:07,020 --> 01:23:10,157
-Can we wrap right now?
-Yeah.

1942
01:23:10,257 --> 01:23:12,092
I mean, there's a couple
of shots we could get.

1943
01:23:12,192 --> 01:23:16,631
Josh, how long
do you need to wrap out?

1944
01:23:16,731 --> 01:23:18,999
It's 11:06.

1945
01:23:19,099 --> 01:23:20,835
-40?
-40.

1946
01:23:20,935 --> 01:23:23,036
[Brendan]
At least. We have to wrap.

1947
01:23:23,136 --> 01:23:24,806
Alright, we're wrapping.

1948
01:23:24,906 --> 01:23:26,373
Alright. You can call it.

1949
01:23:26,473 --> 01:23:28,308
Alright, guys, that's a wrap!

1950
01:23:28,408 --> 01:23:30,678
It's great working with you,
man.

1951
01:23:30,778 --> 01:23:33,213
Thank you, Anthony.
Thank you very much, sir.

1952
01:23:33,313 --> 01:23:34,782
It was a fun project.

1953
01:23:34,883 --> 01:23:36,250
Now we can do our thing.

1954
01:23:36,350 --> 01:23:38,318
-Hi, Anthony.
-Thanks again, Brendan.

1955
01:23:54,067 --> 01:23:56,336
[Anthony, VO] <i>I went into this</i>
<i>hoping that making a bad movie</i>

1956
01:23:56,436 --> 01:23:58,907
<i>was better than</i>
<i>never making a movie at all.</i>

1957
01:23:59,007 --> 01:24:02,309
<i>And now I know for sure,</i>
<i>it is.</i>

1958
01:24:03,811 --> 01:24:06,246
<i>Anyone could have directed</i>
<i>'The Land of Time Forgot'.</i>

1959
01:24:06,346 --> 01:24:09,149
<i>But no-one else was meant to.</i>

1960
01:24:13,655 --> 01:24:14,989
Hi, everyone.

1961
01:24:15,088 --> 01:24:16,991
Hello? Is it turned on?

1962
01:24:17,090 --> 01:24:21,461
-Yeah?
-[cheering and applause]

1963
01:24:25,399 --> 01:24:27,835
Now, this might not be the movie

1964
01:24:27,936 --> 01:24:29,737
I dreamt of making
when I was a kid.

1965
01:24:29,837 --> 01:24:31,204
But I'm proud to share with you

1966
01:24:31,305 --> 01:24:33,808
the first ever Asylum film
made in Australia

1967
01:24:33,908 --> 01:24:35,610
and my first ever film.

1968
01:24:35,710 --> 01:24:38,613
Thank you, and enjoy
'The Land That Time Forgot'.

1969
01:24:38,713 --> 01:24:41,816
[cheering and applause]

1970
01:25:16,718 --> 01:25:18,086
<i>Pinky promise me.</i>

1971
01:25:18,185 --> 01:25:20,555
[laughter]

1972
01:25:27,028 --> 01:25:28,730
<i>Arggh!</i>

1973
01:25:30,798 --> 01:25:32,667
[screams]

1974
01:25:32,767 --> 01:25:34,468
Noo!

1975
01:25:58,726 --> 01:26:00,193
<i>[woman]</i>
<i>We're trying to help you!</i>

1976
01:26:00,293 --> 01:26:01,829
<i>We can help get off the island!</i>

1977
01:26:01,929 --> 01:26:03,531
<i>I hear your little mind games!</i>

1978
01:26:03,631 --> 01:26:06,400
<i>And war has</i>
<i>made me invulnerable to them.</i>

1979
01:26:06,500 --> 01:26:07,902
<i>[laughs]</i>

1980
01:26:08,002 --> 01:26:10,038
<i>Now, I am leaving.</i>

1981
01:26:10,138 --> 01:26:12,372
<i>Auf wiedersehen.</i>

1982
01:26:12,472 --> 01:26:14,609
[laughs]

1983
01:26:23,151 --> 01:26:26,587
[cheering and applause]